{
 "id": "adjective-degrees",
 "title": "Adjectives & Adverbs: Degrees",
 "course": "Adjectives & Adverbs",
 "subtitle": "clārus, clāra, clārum (clear): positive, comparative and superlative",
 "intro": "Adjectives and adverbs have three <b>degrees</b>: positive (<i>clear</i>), comparative (<i>clearer</i>) and superlative (<i>clearest</i>). All three are built on the stem <b>clār-</b>.<ul><li><b>Positive:</b> clārus, clāra, clārum, declined like the 1st and 2nd declensions (equus, puella, forum).</li><li><b>Comparative:</b> stem + <span class=\"end\">-ior</span> (masculine and feminine) / <span class=\"end\">-ius</span> (neuter), then <b>3rd-declension</b> endings on the stem clāriōr-: clāriōris, clāriōrī, clāriōrem, clāriōre; plural clāriōrēs, clāriōrum, clāriōribus. Neuter plural nominative and accusative: clāriōra.</li><li><b>Superlative:</b> stem + <span class=\"end\">-issimus, -issima, -issimum</span>, declined like clārus.</li></ul><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Adverbs:</b> clār<b>ē</b> (clearly), clār<b>ius</b> (more clearly), clār<b>issimē</b> (most clearly, very clearly).</p><p style=\"margin:8px 0 0\"><b>Watch out:</b> <b>clārius</b> is both the neuter comparative adjective and the comparative adverb. Context decides. The comparative can also mean “rather clear” or “too clear”, and the superlative “very clear”.</p>",
 "paradigmNote": "Adverbs: clārē · clārius · clārissimē",
